Add a "Delete browsing data when you close Aerium" toggle to Settings, with
a per-type selection of what gets deleted.

ungoogled-chromium already wires browsing-data-on-exit deletion into
ChromeBrowsingDataLifetimeManager::ClearBrowsingDataForOnExitPolicy() via
extra/ungoogled-chromium/add-flag-to-clear-data-on-exit.patch, but only
behind the #clear-data-on-exit entry in chrome://flags, and that flag wipes
everything including saved passwords and site settings. A flag is not a
feature most people will ever find, and an all-or-nothing wipe is not what
most people want. This exposes the same shutdown deletion as an ordinary
toggle in Settings > Privacy and security, with the eight standard data-type
categories revealed underneath it in a cr-collapse, Brave-style.

It hangs off the existing code path rather than adding a second one: the new
prefs simply widen the condition that already guards the removal and OR into
the mask that is already computed.

Pref design - eight booleans, not a list

  Chromium already has a per-type list pref for exactly this,
  browsing_data::prefs::kClearBrowsingDataOnExitList, which
  GetRemoveMask()/GetOriginTypeMask() consume via NameToPolicyDataType().
  We deliberately do not write to it: it is owned by the
  ClearBrowsingDataOnExit enterprise policy, and a Settings toggle that
  rewrote it would look like the browser overriding managed configuration.
  The two sources are ORed at shutdown, so both keep working independently.

  A *parallel* Aerium list pref was the other option and was rejected. It
  would be policy-shaped for no benefit: settingsPrivate would expose it as
  PrefType::kList, which neither settings-toggle-button nor settings-checkbox
  can bind to, so every checkbox would need hand-written list-membership glue
  in privacy_page.ts - precisely the kind of WebUI plumbing that fails
  silently. Chromium's own Delete-browsing-data dialog models the same eight
  categories as eight booleans (kDeleteBrowsingHistory, kDeleteCache, ...),
  so booleans are the idiomatic shape here: direct pref binding in the
  template, and a straight pref-read-to-mask-OR in C++.

  We do not reuse those kDelete* prefs themselves. They hold the dialog's
  last-used checkbox state; wiring shutdown deletion to them would mean a
  one-off manual "delete cookies" quietly reconfigures what happens on every
  future exit. The new prefs live under browser.clear_data.aerium_on_exit.*.

  The type-to-mask mapping is copied from GetRemoveMask()/GetOriginTypeMask()
  rather than invented, so an identical selection deletes identical things
  whether it came from the toggle or from enterprise policy.

Safety stance

  Defaults reproduce the fixed mask this toggle used before it became
  configurable - browsing history, download history, cookies and other site
  data, cached images and files, and autofill form data - so profiles that
  already had the toggle on keep behaving exactly the same after the upgrade.
  Passwords, site settings and hosted app data default to off. They are
  offered, because the user asked for the choice, but a toggle whose label
  promises a clean slate must not quietly destroy saved sign-ins or per-site
  permissions unless the user actually asks for that.

  Master switch on with every box unchecked is a no-op: the mask is zero, so
  the condition is not satisfied and the pending-deletion pref is not set,
  rather than scheduling a removal of nothing.

Notes

  All nine prefs are added to the settingsPrivate allowlist in prefs_util.cc;
  a pref the Settings WebUI reads or writes that is missing there is refused
  silently and the control simply does nothing.

  privacy_page.ts already composes PrefsMixin, so the writable controls work;
  it only needed the cr-collapse, settings-checkbox and
  settings-toggle-button imports.

  The eight checkbox labels reuse the strings Chromium already registers for
  the Delete-browsing-data dialog (clearBrowsingHistory, clearDownloadHistory,
  clearCookies, clearCache, clearFormData, clearPasswords, clearHostedAppData)
  plus siteSettings, so they arrive already translated in every locale. Only
  the two strings for the master toggle are new.

